WEBVTT

00:00:00,780 --> 00:00:07,700
And everybody, this is Dr. Cutters, and I'm going to talk in this section about what

00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:11,100
other tests could I or should I do?

00:00:11,100 --> 00:00:19,220
So this is for our patients, commonly asked questions trying to cover this to help you

00:00:19,220 --> 00:00:21,780
walk through this decision-making process.

00:00:21,780 --> 00:00:26,500
There's a lot of tests out there that you could do or should do.

00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:30,420
First of all, we're going to cover the tests typically that your oncologist is going to

00:00:30,420 --> 00:00:31,420
recommend.

00:00:31,420 --> 00:00:39,060
So typically, your oncologist is going to recommend CT scans, PET scans, MRIs, maybe bone

00:00:39,060 --> 00:00:44,260
scans, biopsies, more invasive type things.

00:00:44,260 --> 00:00:50,180
We're not going to cover that in this section here, but I will say, when I say these are

00:00:50,180 --> 00:00:54,820
invasive tests, I don't mean that you should avoid them at all costs.

00:00:54,820 --> 00:01:00,900
Many times, they're important to do, especially if you're following a protocol from your

00:01:00,900 --> 00:01:09,460
oncologist, meaning you're doing some sort of standards of care, like radiation or chemotherapy

00:01:09,460 --> 00:01:13,580
or surgery or something, you're going to need to follow their recommendations.

00:01:13,580 --> 00:01:20,180
Now I call them invasive tests because there is some invasiveness to them.

00:01:20,180 --> 00:01:21,700
You do a CT scan.

00:01:21,700 --> 00:01:27,500
Those are sectional tests, like you're going to do an upper bodies CT or abdominal CT.

00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:32,740
Each one of those sectional CTs carries about the rad dose of radiation of about 50 chest

00:01:32,740 --> 00:01:33,740
x, right?

00:01:33,740 --> 00:01:39,980
So they're not something that you should just do to satisfy your curiosity.

00:01:39,980 --> 00:01:46,020
It's something that you do to change your decision-making process and how you're going to treat

00:01:46,020 --> 00:01:47,020
the patient.

00:01:47,020 --> 00:01:49,060
There's times that you have to do that.

00:01:49,060 --> 00:01:51,020
We need to see if this cancer has grown.

00:01:51,020 --> 00:01:57,900
This person has, you know, some signs and symptoms of worsening and we're going to change our

00:01:57,900 --> 00:02:01,020
protocol, maybe change the chemotherapy.

00:02:01,020 --> 00:02:03,540
Should it be true that the cancer's growing?

00:02:03,540 --> 00:02:08,380
We need to do another CT scan to compare with the last CT scan that we did x about a month

00:02:08,380 --> 00:02:09,380
ago.

00:02:09,380 --> 00:02:13,700
Well, the only way to compare that is to compare apples to apples, and that is to do another

00:02:13,780 --> 00:02:19,380
of the same tests that you did prior that brought you to that decision-making process that

00:02:19,380 --> 00:02:21,900
you're following through with right now.

00:02:21,900 --> 00:02:28,020
So there's times that a CT scan, PET scan, MRI is very appropriate.

00:02:28,020 --> 00:02:31,100
You just have to know that there are risks to it.

00:02:31,100 --> 00:02:36,860
Typically MRIs are going to be done with a contrast, die, and typically that's gadolinium, and

00:02:36,860 --> 00:02:39,380
that does build up in your system.

00:02:39,380 --> 00:02:43,820
Some people have very difficult time to talk to if I can have reactions to it.

00:02:43,820 --> 00:02:47,940
So MRIs are not without their risk, either, even though there's no radiation.

00:02:47,940 --> 00:02:51,140
So there's a time and place for invasive tests.

00:02:51,140 --> 00:02:54,660
That's a discussion that we could have individually.

00:02:54,660 --> 00:03:00,180
That's something that definitely you need to do if you're following standards of care.

00:03:00,180 --> 00:03:05,340
But just to do them to satisfy curiosity, if it's not going to change the way it treat the

00:03:05,340 --> 00:03:09,340
patient, I don't know if that's the right decision-making process.

00:03:09,340 --> 00:03:15,140
But what we want to concentrate on in this video is what are the less invasive tests that

00:03:15,140 --> 00:03:19,980
I could do that could help me move my care in a different way.

00:03:19,980 --> 00:03:26,660
That gives me some more data to, you know, change maybe by nutritional protocol, a little

00:03:26,660 --> 00:03:29,900
better, tweak something in my protocol.

00:03:29,900 --> 00:03:34,700
These I would say are less invasive tests, an ultrasound, thermography, labs.

00:03:34,700 --> 00:03:38,700
Of course a lab is invasive because they stick you at the needle to draw blood, but it's certainly

00:03:38,700 --> 00:03:41,460
not, you know, typically harmful.

00:03:41,460 --> 00:03:44,260
There's not going to be any lasting issues with that.

00:03:44,260 --> 00:03:50,540
So let's talk about some of these tests that we more often recommend.

00:03:50,540 --> 00:03:51,940
So the first is an ultrasound.

00:03:51,940 --> 00:03:56,020
No, an ultrasound would only be appropriate depending upon the type of cancer that you have.

00:03:56,020 --> 00:03:59,620
So if you have prostate cancer, they're not going to do an ultrasound to see if your prostate

00:03:59,620 --> 00:04:00,620
cancer's grown.

00:04:00,620 --> 00:04:05,980
But if you have breast cancer, and you haven't had a lump back to be or a mastectomy, and you're

00:04:05,980 --> 00:04:10,140
looking for a change in size, if that may be an appropriate test.

00:04:10,140 --> 00:04:15,140
Now your oncologist may argue that, oh, you get a much better idea of where it's at if

00:04:15,140 --> 00:04:21,820
we do another MRI, but we've had patients that opted out of MRIs because of the contrast

00:04:21,820 --> 00:04:27,740
die, and they wanted to do just follow their cancer with an ultrasound.

00:04:27,740 --> 00:04:30,340
Well, that seems reasonable.

00:04:30,340 --> 00:04:34,700
So an ultrasound could be a reasonable test for things like that.

00:04:34,700 --> 00:04:41,700
An ultrasound could be a reasonable test for bile duct issues, and gallbladder issues,

00:04:41,700 --> 00:04:44,380
and some abdominal type cancers.

00:04:44,380 --> 00:04:51,500
An ultrasound could be a reasonable test to see and guide further care.

00:04:51,500 --> 00:04:56,660
Thermography could be another reasonable test, especially with breast cancer or head and neck

00:04:56,660 --> 00:04:59,940
issues, head and neck cancers.

00:04:59,940 --> 00:05:04,060
But abdominal cancers, no, not really, prostate cancer, no.

00:05:04,060 --> 00:05:10,100
But breast cancer, you could use thermography as a guide to, you know, as the cancer, you

00:05:10,100 --> 00:05:12,260
know, am I improving or not improving?

00:05:12,260 --> 00:05:18,260
The problem is, is that sometimes when you're killing the cancer effectively, either using

00:05:18,260 --> 00:05:24,740
chemotherapy or using the right and nutrition, the cancer can look hotter on thermography

00:05:24,740 --> 00:05:27,980
because you are stimulated in immune response to the cancer.

00:05:27,980 --> 00:05:29,580
That's what we're really trying to do.

00:05:29,580 --> 00:05:34,340
This gets your immune system to kill the cancer, and the thermography can't actually look

00:05:34,340 --> 00:05:35,340
worse.

00:05:35,340 --> 00:05:37,980
So you have to know that with any test.

00:05:37,980 --> 00:05:43,060
That could happen with the CT scanner MRI as well, and the same thing with an ultrasound.

00:05:43,060 --> 00:05:48,500
If you're effectively killing the cancer, and your immune system is, is populating a reaction

00:05:48,500 --> 00:05:55,340
to the tumor, the tumor can look larger for a period of time because you're causing this

00:05:55,340 --> 00:05:59,060
state of local inflammation as your immune system is attacking it.

00:05:59,060 --> 00:06:08,660
So you can get what's called a pseudo enlargement of a tumor or a pseudo-worsity of a cancer because

00:06:08,660 --> 00:06:12,700
it's your body's actually doing what it's supposed to be doing what we want it to do.

00:06:12,700 --> 00:06:16,300
So you just have to understand that with any testing.

00:06:16,300 --> 00:06:18,580
The next is a laboratory testing.

00:06:18,580 --> 00:06:24,940
The laboratory test is something we really like to see, and it's some lab tests your

00:06:24,940 --> 00:06:27,500
oncologist will recommend.

00:06:27,500 --> 00:06:32,060
So that's what we're going to spend more of our time on this video, and this is where

00:06:32,060 --> 00:06:35,900
the slide gets a little bit more comprehensive.

00:06:35,900 --> 00:06:41,700
So laboratory testing could be broken down into three categories, some of your standard tests,

00:06:41,700 --> 00:06:47,860
and then we could call them some specific lab tests that are specific to cancer, and then

00:06:47,860 --> 00:06:50,180
some functional labs.

00:06:50,180 --> 00:06:58,780
So the standard labs that your oncologist or your general practitioner may already be doing

00:06:58,780 --> 00:07:04,100
are things like a CDC, that's a complete blood count, or a complete metabolic panel, which

00:07:04,100 --> 00:07:12,300
is going to measure your alkaline phosphid, your liver, function tests, and understand different

00:07:12,300 --> 00:07:14,140
labs have different names for these.

00:07:14,140 --> 00:07:19,220
There's all sorts of, there's no standardization necessarily for names of labs, except for

00:07:19,220 --> 00:07:23,100
some of the general ones like CBC and a complete metabolic panel.

00:07:23,100 --> 00:07:29,100
Then there's a comprehensive lipid panel where we get into the breakdown and different

00:07:29,100 --> 00:07:34,700
labs have different tests for that, where you get into not just your LDLs and HDLs, but

00:07:34,700 --> 00:07:40,820
you get into oxidized LDLs and HDLs and things, and that gives you a much better idea of

00:07:40,900 --> 00:07:45,340
how the fats of your body are doing, and typically we're not interested in that so much

00:07:45,340 --> 00:07:46,900
with cancer.

00:07:46,900 --> 00:07:51,940
So comprehensive lipid panel wouldn't be really anything you'd really necessarily do with

00:07:51,940 --> 00:07:56,020
cancer, except that, just because you have cancer doesn't mean you don't have other things

00:07:56,020 --> 00:07:57,020
going on.

00:07:57,020 --> 00:08:01,940
You have the right to have more than one problem at one time that you may want to address.

00:08:01,940 --> 00:08:07,060
So that may be something, and it is something that we have ordered or told people to order

00:08:07,060 --> 00:08:08,380
in the past.

00:08:08,380 --> 00:08:10,700
It's like your thyroid panel.

00:08:10,700 --> 00:08:15,580
Well, if you have liver cancer, colon cancer, what do you care about your thyroid panel?

00:08:15,580 --> 00:08:20,140
Well, to be honest, you should care about it, because if you do have hypothyroid or

00:08:20,140 --> 00:08:26,460
Hashimoto's or something like that, that could really affect your ability to enlist it

00:08:26,460 --> 00:08:32,780
in a beauty response if your thyroid is low, and your energy is going to be low, you're

00:08:32,780 --> 00:08:36,700
going to have fatigue issues, that's just quality of life issues.

00:08:36,700 --> 00:08:40,340
It's not good to be walking around with low thyroid, worse to be walking around with

00:08:40,340 --> 00:08:41,340
high thyroid.

00:08:41,340 --> 00:08:45,840
So it's something that you may want to address, and it's something just because again, you

00:08:45,840 --> 00:08:50,020
have cancer doesn't mean we can't look at other things too.

00:08:50,020 --> 00:08:56,180
Other specific labs to cancer would be mainly just the tumor marker tests, and we'll get

00:08:56,180 --> 00:08:58,060
into detail with that.

00:08:58,060 --> 00:09:02,820
And tumor marker tests have a place, and you have to understand that place.

